@page "/Error"
@using System.Diagnostics

<PageTitle>Stumpty — Something went wrong</PageTitle>

@* The .NET template's version of this page told visitors to set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT
   and restart the app — developer instructions shown to whoever came for a riddle. It
   also styled its headings with Bootstrap's text-danger, which stopped existing when
   Bootstrap was removed (PRD 09).

   Only reachable in production: Program.cs wires UseExceptionHandler behind
   !IsDevelopment(). It catches failures during the initial HTTP request — a corrupt
   teasers.json at startup, say. Errors on a live circuit surface in #blazor-error-ui
   instead, so this page is the cold-start case only. *@
<div class="oad-static">
    <div class="oad-banner">Something went wrong</div>

    <div class="oad-box err-box">
        <p class="err-lead">
            Sorry — the site hit a problem loading that page. Nothing you did caused it.
        </p>
        <p class="err-sub">
            Try again in a moment. If it keeps happening, it's on our side and we're
            probably already looking at it.
        </p>

        <a class="err-home" href="">Back to the Challenge of the day</a>

        @* Kept, quietly: it's the one thing that makes a report actionable if someone
           does get in touch. Not framed as something they need to act on. *@
        @if (ShowRequestId)
        {
            <p class="err-ref">Reference: <code>@RequestId</code></p>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private string? RequestId { get; set; }
    private bool ShowRequestId => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(RequestId);

    protected override void OnInitialized() =>
        RequestId = Activity.Current?.Id ?? HttpContext?.TraceIdentifier;
}
